{\b\fs20 Lemur records conversations. Please read this first.}\par
Lemur records your calls and meetings (your microphone and the sound of the call) and sends the recordings to the transcription and AI servers that you, or whoever looks after your servers, set up. It sends nothing to Lemur's authors, and nothing to a cloud service unless you point it at one.\par
{\b Tell people you're recording.} In many places it is against the law to record a conversation without everyone's agreement. In some US states, such as California and Illinois, every person in the conversation must agree. Your employer may have its own rules too. Lemur reminds you each time a recording starts; telling people is up to you.\par
{\b Voices are personal data.} Recognising people by their voice is off until you turn it on. Only turn it on with the agreement of the people you record, and remove anyone who asks.\par
{\b Check before you rely on it.} Transcripts and AI summaries can be wrong: names, numbers, who agreed to what. The note keeps the recording, so you can listen to any line.\par
{\b No warranty.} Lemur is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind. You use it at your own risk.\par
{\b Other people's work.} Lemur includes the .NET runtime (MIT), NAudio (MIT) and Markdig (BSD-2-Clause). The speech and AI models run on your own servers, under their own licences; the help pages list them.\par
